@kind(writ)

=[ Writ: workspace root ]

Rules the whole repository obeys.

Scope is this directory and everything under it. A narrower rule lives in a
`.writ.tmt` closer to what it governs; read every `.writ.tmt` from a file up
to here, the way `.gitignore` is read.

Only the author edits this file. An agent that finds a rule in its way
proposes an edit here and stops. It does not work around the rule, and it
does not relax the rule to fit the change it wanted to make.

==[ crate-layering ]

@rule(crate-layering){
  guard: { twrit: layers }

  layers: {
    2: list( "app" )
    1: list( "crates/sisho-tools", "crates/sisho-vault", "crates/sisho-serve", "crates/sisho-counter" )
    0: list( "crates/sisho-run" )
  }
}

Every workspace member belongs to exactly one layer, and depends only on
layers strictly below its own. Same-layer dependencies are allowed;
upward ones are not.

Layer 0 runs commands and knows no tool by name. Layer 1 holds what `sisho`
knows about the core tools (`sisho-tools`) and what it implements itself
(`sisho-vault`, `sisho-serve`, `sisho-counter`). Layer 2 is the program.

`sisho-serve` is its own crate so that the HTTP stack stays out of the
build of every other command. `sisho-counter` is its own crate so that
the TUI stack stays out of the core CLI build.

Dev-dependencies are exempt.

---[ Why ]---

The order is what keeps a change local. Adding a tool means touching
`sisho-tools` and the program, not the runner. An edge running upward is
the first sign that a layer has started to know what it should not.

==[ tools-by-subprocess ]

@rule(tools-by-subprocess){
  guard: { twrit: pure }

  pure: {
    crates: [ "crates/sisho-run", "crates/sisho-tools" ]
    forbid: [ "std::fs", "std::net" ]
    allow-external: list( "jobserver", "libc", "getrandom", "cfg-if", "r-efi" )
  }
}

`tomet`, `twrit` and `tandr` are called as subprocesses, never linked as
crates. The two crates that run and describe those calls depend on no
external crate except what is in the allowlist: `jobserver` and what it brings
in (`libc`, `getrandom`, `cfg-if`, `r-efi`). None of it is a `tomet-*` crate;
a `jobserver` update may change the list.

Only what `sisho` implements itself -- `sisho-vault`, `sisho-serve` --
uses the `tomet` crates, through git dependencies.

---[ Why ]---

If `twrit` pins one revision of `tomet` and `sisho` pins another, the same
type exists in two versions and nothing links. A subprocess has no types to
disagree about. The versions are kept in step by the flake, which pins the
tools as one set.

==[ process-door ]

@rule(process-door){
  guard: { twrit: door }

  door: {
    names: list( "std::process" )
    within: list( "crates/*" )
    allowed: list( "crates/sisho-run" )
  }
}

A library crate starts a process only if it is named above. Programs are
out of scope: the program decides what to run through the crates below it.

---[ Why ]---

`sisho` is not a task runner and executes no command it was not written to
know. Keeping the one place that can start a process small makes that
checkable: a new crate that starts reaching for `std::process` is the
failure, and it shows up here rather than in review.

==[ tmtroot-output-not-edited ]

@rule(tmtroot-output-not-edited){
  guard: { runs: "just docs-check" }
}

A file exported from @dir(tmtroot/) is generated. Do not hand-edit it. Edit
the source and re-export.
